      menuItem.dom.link.addEventListener("focus", () => {
        this.focusState = "self";
        this.currentChild = index;
      });
    });

    // Close the menu when focus leaves the menu entirely (e.g., tabbing out)
    this.dom.menu.addEventListener("focusout", (event) => {
      if (!this.dom.menu.contains(event.relatedTarget)) {
        // Focus left the menu
        if (this.isTopLevel && this.elements.controller && this.elements.controller.isOpen) {
          this.elements.controller.close();
        }
        this.focusState = "none";
      }
    });
  }"
    }
  ],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "When a keyboard user tabs out of the navigation, the Shop dropdown will close (aria-expanded becomes false and the submenu is hidden), satisfying the requirement that the dropdown does not cover the page after navigation.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Potential risk of closing the menu when focus temporarily leaves due to programmatic focus shifts or when using assistive technology that moves focus outside unexpectedly; however, the check ensures the menu only closes when focus moves to a node not descended from the menu, which is appropriate.",
    "Ensure that submenus are not inadvertently closed when moving between sibling items (they remain inside). Risk low."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "Tabbing from the Shop button into the dropdown and then to the Search input results in the Shop button having aria-expanded="false" and the Plants link being hidden.",
    "Pressing Escape while the dropdown is open closes it and returns focus to the Shop button (existing guard).",
    "Clicking the Shop button still opens and closes the dropdown correctly.",
    "Navigating within the dropdown with arrow keys (if optional support enabled) continues to work.",
    "Menu remains open when moving focus between menu items (e.g., from Shop button to Plants link to Tools link)."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The journey:

Step 0: seek #shop (the Shop button)
Step 1: press Enter -> expects aria-expanded="true" on #shop and visible #plants (dropdown opens)
Step 2: seek #search (tab out of navigation to search input) -> expects aria-expanded="false" on #shop and #plants invisible

Observed failure: At step 2, after tabbing to #search, they observed:
- #shop aria-expanded attribute observed "true" (but expected false)
- #plants visible observed true (expected false)

Thus the dropdown remains open when focus leaves the navigation via tab to search.

In the observed state, we see focus moved to #search input, but the Shop button still has aria-expanded="true" and the plants link is visible.

So the issue: When focus leaves the menu via tab (keyboard navigation), the dropdown should close (aria-expanded false, hide submenu). Currently it stays open.

Looking at the code: The menu is a disclosure menu built with the disclosed pattern. Likely missing a blur handler that closes the menu when focus leaves the menu (or when focus moves to an element outside the menu). In the base menu, there is a blur method? Let's examine.

From _baseMenu.js, we have methods: blur(), focus(), etc. There's a _handleFocus() that sets focusState to self when a menu item gets focus. There's also blurChildren etc.

But we need to close the menu when focus leaves the menu entirely (i.e., when focus goes to an element not in the menu). In the observed flow: focus goes from #shop (button) to #plants (link inside dropdown) to #tools (link) to #search (input outside nav). Actually the focusEvents sequence shows:

[
 {"from":"body","sequence":0,"tag":"a","to":"#home"},
 {"from":"#home","sequence":1,"tag":"a","to":"#contact"},
 {"from":"#contact","sequence":2,"tag":"button","to":"#shop"},
 {"from":"#shop","sequence":3,"tag":"a","to":"#plants"},
 {"from":"#plants","sequence":4,"tag":"a","to":"#tools"},
 {"from":"#tools","sequence":5,"tag":"input","to":"#search"}
]

So after focusing #tools (last link in dropdown), pressing Tab moves focus to #search (outside the nav). At that point, we need to close the dropdown.

Where should we handle this? Possibly in the base menu's blur method or via a focusout event on the menu container.

Look at _baseMenu.js: there is a blur() method that sets focusState = "none" and if shouldFocus then dom.menu.blur(); but that only blurs the menu element itself, not close submenus.

There is also blurChildren() that blurs all menu items and submenus.

We need to close the dropdown when focus leaves the menu (i.e., when focus goes to an element not inside the menu). Could add a focusout listener on the menu element that checks if the relatedTarget is outside the menu, then close.

Alternatively, we could rely on the existing blur handler in _handleFocus? Actually _handleFocus adds focus listener to each menu item link to set focusState=self and currentChild. No blur listener.

We could add a blur listener to the menu element (or to each menu item) that when focus leaves, we close open submenus.

Given the architecture, maybe we should add a "focusout" event listener on the menu's container (or the menu itself) that triggers closeChildren and blur.

Let's look at where the controller (the shop button) is set up. In BaseMenu.initialize(), if isTopLevel and controller and container exist, they create a BaseMenuToggle controlling the menu, and set its aria-controls. The toggle likely handles open/close via click/keydown/keyup.

But we need to close when focus leaves the menu via tab.

Potential fix: In _handleFocus, besides setting focusState=self, we could also check if the previously focused item was the last item and now focusing outside? Not reliable.

Better: Add a focusout listener on the menu element (this.dom.menu) that when focus leaves, we close children and blur controller.

Let's examine if there is already a focusout listener somewhere: I don't see one.

Thus we can edit _baseMenu.js to add a focusout listener in _handleFocus or a new method _handleFocusOut.

We need to make minimal changes: add a listener for "focusout" on this.dom.menu that checks if the relatedTarget is not inside the menu, then closeChildren and blur controller (if any). Also set focusState to "none".

But we must ensure we don't break existing behavior for submenus etc.

Simplify: On focusout of the menu, if the menu is open (i.e., controller.isOpen?), close it.

Since this is a disclosure menu (the shop button controls the dropdown), we can close the controller if it exists and isOpen.

Thus in _baseMenu.js, after setting up focus listeners, we can add:

this.dom.menu.addEventListener("focusout", (event) => {
  if (!this.dom.menu.contains(event.relatedTarget)) {
    // Focus left the menu
    if (this.isTopLevel && this.elements.controller && this.elements.controller.isOpen) {
      this.elements.controller.close();
    }
    this.focusState = "none";
  }
});

But need to ensure we don't close when moving focus within the menu (e.g., from button to link inside). Since relatedTarget will be the element we are leaving; if we move from button to link inside menu, relatedTarget is the button, which is inside the menu, so contains returns true, we won't close. Good.

When moving from last link inside menu to outside (search), relatedTarget is the link (inside menu), still inside? Wait: focusout occurs on the element losing focus. The listener is on the menu element (the ul#nav-menu). When focus leaves the menu (goes to search), the focusout event fires on the menu element? Actually focusout bubbles; if we attach listener to the menu element, it will fire when focus leaves any descendant and goes outside, because the menu element itself does not lose focus; focusout on the menu element fires when focus leaves the menu element or any of its descendants and moves to a node that is not a descendant of the menu element. The relatedTarget is the element that focus is going to (the new target). So if focus moves from #tools (inside menu) to #search (outside), the focusout event on the menu element will have relatedTarget = #search (outside). Contains check: this.dom.menu.contains(event.relatedTarget) will be false, so we close.

If focus moves from #shop button to #plants link (both inside menu), relatedTarget is #plants (inside), contains true, we don't close.

If focus moves from #plants to #tools (both inside), same.

If focus moves from #tools back to #shop (still inside), relatedTarget is #shop (inside), contains true.

If focus moves from #shop to #home (outside nav but still in same nav? Actually #home is also a link in the nav list, but it's outside the #shop button's submenu but still inside the nav menu? The nav menu ul includes all items: home, contact, shop (with submenu). So #home is also a descendant of the nav menu ul. So moving from shop to home would keep focus inside the nav menu ul, not close.

Thus the logic works.

